Problem 4: Time-Intensive DOM Management

================================================================================

DOM work can involve:

1. Find/select the required element
          ↓
2. Modify the element
          ↓
3. Browser processes the DOM/style changes
          ↓
4. Browser may perform layout and/or paint work
          ↓
5. Updated UI appears


Repeated or poorly structured DOM operations can increase:

Area
-----------------------------
CPU work
Memory usage
Rendering/layout work
Paint operations
Potential battery usage

Example: Updating a List
// Vanilla JavaScript

const list =
  document.getElementById('myList');

// Clear the list
list.innerHTML = '';

// Add 100 items
for (let i = 1; i <= 100; i++) {

  const item =
    document.createElement('li');

  item.textContent = `Item ${i}`;

  list.appendChild(item);
}

What Happens?
Step | Action
-----|----------------------------------------
1    | Find the list element
2    | Clear its existing content
3    | Loop 100 times
4    | Create each li element
5    | Set each item's text
6    | Append each item to the DOM


Repeated direct DOM changes can become inefficient if not carefully designed or batched.

