PART 4: WHY REACT IS THE SOLUTION

================================================================================

What is React?

React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces.

React helps with:

Feature                  | Benefit
-------------------------|----------------------------------------------
State-driven UI          | UI reflects application state
Re-rendering             | UI recalculates when state changes
DOM handling             | React manages committed DOM updates
Components               | UI is divided into reusable pieces
Declarative Code         | Describe what UI should look like
Maintainability          | Organizes complex application interfaces


================================================================================

HOW REACT SOLVES THE PROBLEMS

================================================================================

Solution 1: State-Driven UI Updates

React state allows data changes to trigger new renders.

USER ACTION
     ↓
EVENT HANDLER
     ↓
STATE UPDATE
     ↓
RENDER
     ↓
REACT UPDATES UI


This reduces the need to manually synchronize state variables with individual DOM elements.

React Example: Todo App
import React, { useState } from 'react';

export default function TodoApp() {

  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('all');

  const addTodo = (text) => {

    setTodos([
      ...todos,
      {
        id: Date.now(),
        text,
        completed: false
      }
    ]);
  };

  const deleteTodo = (id) => {

    setTodos(
      todos.filter(t => t.id !== id)
    );
  };

  const toggleTodo = (id) => {

    setTodos(
      todos.map(t =>
        t.id === id
          ? {
              ...t,
              completed: !t.completed
            }
          : t
      )
    );
  };

  const filteredTodos =
    todos.filter(t => {

      if (filter === 'active')
        return !t.completed;

      if (filter === 'completed')
        return t.completed;

      return true;
    });

  return (
    <div>

      <input
        id="todoInput"
        type="text"
      />

      <button
        onClick={() => {

          const input =
            document.getElementById('todoInput');

          addTodo(input.value);

          input.value = '';
        }}
      >
        Add Todo
      </button>

      <ul>
        {filteredTodos.map(todo => (

          <li
            key={todo.id}
            style={{
              textDecoration:
                todo.completed
                  ? 'line-through'
                  : 'none'
            }}
          >

            {todo.text}

            <button
              onClick={() =>
                toggleTodo(todo.id)
              }
            >
              Toggle
            </button>

            <button
              onClick={() =>
                deleteTodo(todo.id)
              }
            >
              Delete
            </button>

          </li>

        ))}
      </ul>

      <button
        onClick={() => setFilter('all')}
      >
        All
      </button>

      <button
        onClick={() => setFilter('active')}
      >
        Active
      </button>

      <button
        onClick={() => setFilter('completed')}
      >
        Completed
      </button>

    </div>
  );
}

Code Explanation
Code / Concept                    | Explanation
----------------------------------|------------------------------------------
useState([])                      | Creates todos state
setTodos(...)                     | Updates todos state
useState('all')                   | Creates filter state
setFilter(...)                    | Changes selected filter
setTodos([...todos, newTodo])     | Adds a new todo
todos.filter(...)                 | Filters/removes items
todos.map(...)                    | Creates updated todo collection
filteredTodos                     | Derived list based on filter
filteredTodos.map(...)            | Renders todos from application data

Important Flow
setTodos(...)
     ↓
TODOS STATE CHANGES
     ↓
COMPONENT RUNS AGAIN
     ↓
filteredTodos RECALCULATED
     ↓
JSX DESCRIBES NEW UI
     ↓
REACT UPDATES UI

Advantages
Advantage
------------------------------------------
Cleaner state-to-UI relationship
Easier application flow
UI updates from state changes
Less manual DOM synchronization
Reusable UI structure


Note: This lesson's React example still uses document.getElementById() for the input. In a more React-style implementation, the input value would typically also be managed through React state or a ref.

