Solution 2: Efficient UI / DOM Updates

================================================================================

React maintains an internal representation of the UI and uses reconciliation to determine which committed DOM changes are needed.

This is commonly explained using the Virtual DOM concept.

STATE CHANGES
     ↓
REACT RENDERS NEW UI DESCRIPTION
     ↓
RECONCILIATION
     ↓
DETERMINE REQUIRED CHANGES
     ↓
UPDATE REAL DOM

Vanilla JavaScript Example
// Rebuilds the list

list.innerHTML = '';

todos.forEach(todo => {
  // Create and add each item
});

React Example
{todos.map(todo => (
  <li key={todo.id}>
    {todo.text}
  </li>
))}


The key helps React identify list items across renders.

Vanilla Example                 | React Example
--------------------------------|-------------------------------------
Clear list manually             | Describe list using JSX
Create elements manually        | map() data into components/elements
Append elements manually        | React commits required DOM changes
Manual DOM synchronization      | State drives rendered output


